-- title:
--   Appendix B — the four examples
-- statement:
--   For each of the four examples of Appendix B (messages of 2, 0, 32 and 63 bytes), with $m$, $r$ and $\mathrm{AES}_k(n)$ as printed: the printed $r$ satisfies the conditions of p. 4 (`IsClamped`), the integers $c_1, \dots, c_q$ (`chunk`) are as printed, $\underline{m}(r) \bmod 2^{130} - 5$ (`poly`, evaluated at $r = r[0] + 2^8 r[1] + \dots + 2^{120} r[15]$) is as printed, and $\mathrm{Poly1305}_r(m, \mathrm{AES}_k(n))$ (`poly1305`, with the printed $\mathrm{AES}_k(n)$ as the 16-byte string) is as printed. The rows $k$ and $n$ are not used: AES is not computed, and the printed $\mathrm{AES}_k(n)$ is taken as given.
-- source:
--   Bernstein, D. J., The Poly1305-AES message-authentication code, in: Fast Software Encryption (FSE 2005), Lecture Notes in Computer Science 3557, Springer, 2005, 32–49, https://doi.org/10.1007/11502760_3 (author's version of 2005-03-29: https://cr.yp.to/mac/poly1305-20050329.pdf), p. 18, Appendix B (Examples)

